ner number** in this question.","explanation":"The owner is several mice; the nests names what is owned.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"owner-number","contextKey":"owner-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"04690c535421115a9dbe9db439d697dd737a31e49124e9e80dfb5cee00d7107f","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **owner number**. The owner phrase determines apostrophe placement. The owned object can itself be singular or plural. Names ending in s have additional conventions outside this bank.","The owner is several mice; the nests names what is owned.","Choose **several mice**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**an unspecified place** is not the owner identified before the possessive mark. The owner is several mice; the nests names what is owned.","B":"**an unspecified time** is not the owner identified before the possessive mark. The owner is several mice; the nests names what is owned.","C":"The owner is several mice; the nests names what is owned.","D":"**the nests** is not the owner identified before the possessive mark. The owner is several mice; the nests names what is owned."}}},{"id":"13eb691b-87e3-561d-a995-a7d50a96e273","topicSlug":"possessive-s","level":"A1","prompt":"Which rule explains the spelling of “the mice''s” in “the mice''s nests”?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Add only an apostrophe to a plural ending in s"},{"id":"B","text":"Add apostrophe plus s to a singular owner"},{"id":"C","text":"Remove the apostrophe to show possession"},{"id":"D","text":"Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"For a regular plural already ending in s, add only an apostrophe. For an irregular plural without final s, add apostrophe plus s. Focus on **apostrophe rule** in this question.","explanation":"The mice refers to several mice. Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"apostrophe-rule","contextKey":"owner-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-possessive-s"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"7fe4d0381814b9e1e72964f9507d76b48a0136d09be0013bf21f4ea0c978c119","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **apostrophe rule**. For a regular plural already ending in s, add only an apostrophe. For an irregular plural without final s, add apostrophe plus s.","The mice refers to several mice. Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s.","Choose **Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This rule does not match the owner’s singular/plural form and final letter. The mice refers to several mice. Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s.","B":"This rule does not match the owner’s singular/plural form and final letter. The mice refers to several mice. Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s.","C":"This rule does not match the owner’s singular/plural form and final letter. The mice refers to several mice. Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s.","D":"The mice refers to several mice. Add apostrophe plus s to an irregular plural without final s."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Possessive marking connects an owner or related participant with a noun. It can express literal ownership, family relationships, authorship, time and other associations. First identify the owner and whether its noun is singular or plural; then place the apostrophe accordingly."],"rules":[{"title":"One owner","explanation":"Add apostrophe plus s to a singular owner noun in the forms practised here. The owner comes before the thing owned.","examples":["the explorer’s compass","the rabbit’s hutch","the **student’s** answer = one student"],"pattern":"one teacher → the teacher**’s** desk","paragraphs":["A singular owner normally takes apostrophe + s. The owned noun follows the marked owner: the teacher’s desk. The number of possessions does not determine the apostrophe: the teacher’s books still has one teacher. Names ending in s have style-dependent conventions, so follow the stated style."]},{"title":"Plural owners","explanation":"For a regular plural already ending in s, add only an apostrophe. For an irregular plural without final s, add apostrophe plus s.","examples":["the explorers’ compasses","the children’s drawings","the **students’** answers = several students"],"pattern":"several teachers → the teachers**’** desks","paragraphs":["A regular plural ending in s takes an apostrophe after that s. An irregular plural without final s takes ’s: children’s, women’s, people’s. The contrast is about the owner’s form, not whether the thing owned is singular or plural."]},{"title":"Read the whole phrase","explanation":"The owner phrase determines apostrophe placement. The owned object can itself be singular or plural. Names ending in s have additional conventions outside this bank.","examples":["one student’s notebooks","several students’ classroom","the **children’s** playground = several children"],"pattern":"children → the children**’s** books","paragraphs":["The possessive phrase normally places the owner before the owned noun. An of phrase is another way of expressing many relationships, especially with longer or inanimate noun phrases. It is not always stylistically interchangeable, so learn common patterns."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Its, hers, yours, ours and theirs are possessive forms without apostrophes. It’s is a contraction. In coordinated possession, Sam and Jo’s house often suggests joint possession; Sam’s and Jo’s houses suggests separate relationships. The apostrophe attaches to the owner phrase.","examples":["the **student’s** answer = one student","the **students’** answers = several students","the **children’s** playground = several children"]}],"recap":["one teacher → the teacher**’s** desk","several teachers → the teachers**’** desks","children → the children**’s** books"]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["articles"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
